the Supreme Court just made it legal for  every gay couple in America to get  married and marriage is a solid  financial option for everything from  employment benefits and estate taxes to  discounts on insurance but there are  some hidden costs to getting married to  consider these three things number one  income taxes for a couple where both  partners are well paid the marriage  penalty can be tens of thousands of  dollars here's an extreme example let's  say you and your partner each make 300  thousand dollars a year that means  you're each paying a top tax rate of 33%  but if you get married your combined six  hundred thousand dollars is taxed at a  top marginal rate of 39.6% that can cost  you more than twenty thousand dollars a  year  number two Roth IRAs for couples that  make over a hundred and ninety three  thousand dollars a year you might make  too much to be eligible to open Roth  IRAs the IRS prevents wealthier people  from taking advantage of the Roth IRA  tax break number three student loans  marriage can make it harder to qualify  for college or graduate school financial  aid for example the government's income  based repayment program limits your  student loan payments to a percentage of  your income if you make $40,000 a year  and have a hundred thousand dollars in  education debt your payment with IBR  could be two hundred and eighty dollars  a month but if you marry someone also  making forty thousand dollars your  payment can jump above seven hundred  dollars a month if your spouse makes  more than that you may stop qualifying  for the program  of course none of these hidden costs  outweigh the joy and benefits of  marrying a significant other love after  all has no price tag
 

